content:
Absolutely! I'll give you the most important JavaScript code for building the framework of CS409
MP3, so you can understand how to organize the backend and then implement each feature
yourself.

We'll use JavaScript + Node.js + Express + Mongoose + MongoDB.

Since your GitHub repository already contains a more complete server.js , treat this as a
simplified learning template. Don't overwrite your existing implementation.

Step 1 — Set up the project

Open your MP3 folder in VS Code and run:


node -v
npm install


Your assignment uses Node.js 24 LTS.

Your basic file structure looks like this:


mp3/
├── server.js         # Main backend code
├── package.json      # Dependencies
├── .env              # MongoDB connection string
├── .gitignore        # Protect private files
├── submit.js         # Autograder
└── README.md         # Assignment instructions
Step 2 — Create the Express server

This is the basic structure of a Node.js backend.


// 1. Load dependencies
require("dotenv").config();


const express = require("express");
const mongoose = require("mongoose");


// 2. Create the Express application
const app = express();


// 3. Allow Express to read JSON request bodies
app.use(express.json());


// 4. Create a test route
app.get("/", (req, res) => {
    res.json({
        message: "Server is running!",
        data: null
    });
});


// 5. Get the port
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;


Understand these important lines

const app = express() creates the backend application.

app.use(express.json()) allows your server to receive JSON information from clients.

app.get("/", ...) creates an endpoint that responds when somebody visits your server's root
URL.

You will eventually define all ten required endpoints using the same general structure.

Step 3 — Define your database models

MongoDB stores information as documents.
Mongoose schemas define what those documents should contain.


User schema


const userSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
    name: {
        type: String,
        required: true
    },


    email: {
        type: String,
        required: true,
        unique: true
    },


    pendingTasks: {
        type: [String],
        default: []
    },


    dateCreated: {
        type: Date,
        default: Date.now
    }
});


const User = mongoose.model("User", userSchema);


Important concepts:

    type defines the data type.
    required: true indicates a mandatory field.
    unique: true tells Mongoose to create a unique database index; it is not, by itself, complete
    request validation.
    default supplies a value when one is not provided.


Task schema


const taskSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
    name: {
        type: String,
        required: true
    },


    description: {
        type: String,
        default: ""
    },


    deadline: {
        type: Date,
        required: true
    },


    completed: {
        type: Boolean,
        default: false
    },


    assignedUser: {
        type: String,
        default: ""
    },


    assignedUserName: {
        type: String,
        default: "unassigned"
    },


    dateCreated: {
        type: Date,
        default: Date.now
    }
});


const Task = mongoose.model("Task", taskSchema);


These two schemas are fundamental because almost every API operation interacts with one or both
of these models.

Remember that schemas alone do not enforce the assignment's two-way reference rules. You will
implement those in your route logic.
Step 4 — Learn the most important CRUD patterns

CRUD stands for Create, Read, Update, Delete.

Here are the Mongoose operations you will repeatedly use.


Create a document


const user = new User({
    name: "Alice",
    email: "alice@example.com"
});


await user.save();


new User(...) creates a document object, and .save() stores it in MongoDB.


Read documents


// Find all users
const users = await User.find();


// Find one user
const user = await User.findById(userId);


Update a document


const user = await User.findById(userId);


if (user) {
    user.name = "Bob";
    await user.save();
}


Delete a document


await User.deleteOne({ _id: userId });
These examples teach the basic database operations. For MP3, you need additional validation,
status codes, and synchronization before using these operations in complete API handlers.

Step 5 — Understand how to build API routes

Here is a simple example that connects Express with Mongoose:


app.get("/api/users", async (req, res) => {
    try {
        const users = await User.find();


        res.status(200).json({
            message: "OK",
            data: users
        });


    } catch (error) {
        res.status(500).json({
            message: "Unable to retrieve users.",
            data: null
        });
    }
});


Let's break it down:

  1. app.get() defines the route.
  2. async allows asynchronous database operations.
  3. User.find() retrieves users from MongoDB.
  4. await waits for the database result.
  5. res.status(200) indicates the request succeeded.
  6. res.json() sends the result back to the client.

This is only a basic example. Your completed GET /api/users must also support the
assignment's query parameters.


Your full route structure

You can use this as a planning template:
// ============= USERS =============


// GET all users
app.get("/api/users", async (req, res) => {
    // TODO: Retrieve users and apply query parameters
});


// POST create user
app.post("/api/users", async (req, res) => {
    // TODO: Validate input, create user,
    // and synchronize pending tasks
});


// GET one user
app.get("/api/users/:id", async (req, res) => {
    // TODO: Find user by ID
});


// PUT replace user
app.put("/api/users/:id", async (req, res) => {
    // TODO: Validate and replace user,
    // then synchronize related tasks
});


// DELETE user
app.delete("/api/users/:id", async (req, res) => {
    // TODO: Unassign related tasks,
    // then delete user
});

// ============= TASKS =============


// GET all tasks
app.get("/api/tasks", async (req, res) => {
    // TODO: Retrieve tasks with filtering,
    // sorting, pagination, etc.
});


// POST create task
app.post("/api/tasks", async (req, res) => {
    // TODO: Validate and create task,
    // then synchronize assigned user
});


// GET one task
app.get("/api/tasks/:id", async (req, res) => {
    // TODO: Find task by ID
});


// PUT replace task
app.put("/api/tasks/:id", async (req, res) => {
    // TODO: Replace task and update
    // related user references
});


// DELETE task
app.delete("/api/tasks/:id", async (req, res) => {
    // TODO: Remove task from user's
    // pendingTasks and delete it
});


This gives you the overall architecture. The handlers are intentionally unfinished and will need real
implementations before the server can pass the autograder.

Step 6 — Connect to MongoDB and start the server

Place this near the bottom of your backend file, after defining your schemas and routes.


async function startServer() {
    try {
        await mongoose.connect(process.env.MONGODB_URI);


        app.listen(PORT, () => {
            console.log(`Server running on port ${PORT}`);
        });


    } catch (error) {
        console.error("Database connection failed.");
        process.exit(1);
    }
}


startServer();
Your .env  file should contain a real MongoDB Atlas connection string:


MONGODB_URI=mongodb+srv://YOUR_USERNAME:YOUR_PASSWORD@YOUR_CLUSTER.mongodb.net/mp3


Replace those placeholders with your actual database credentials. Keep .env private and make
sure it is ignored by Git.

Then start your server:


npm start


You should see something similar to:


Server running on port 3000


Step 7 — Three more JavaScript concepts you need

A. req.body — Read client data

For a POST request:


const { name, email } = req.body;


This extracts the name and email fields from the request body.

For example, if the client sends:


{
  "name": "Alice",
  "email": "alice@example.com"
}


Then name becomes "Alice" and email becomes "alice@example.com" .
B. req.params — Read URL parameters


app.get("/example/:id", (req, res) => {
    const id = req.params.id;


    res.json({ message: "OK", data: id });
});


If the client requests /example/123 , then req.params.id is "123" .


C. req.query — Read query parameters


app.get("/example", (req, res) => {
    const limit = req.query.limit;


    res.json({ message: "OK", data: limit });
});


If the client requests:


/example?limit=10


Then req.query.limit contains the query parameter value. Remember that query parameters
need parsing and validation before being used as numbers or JSON objects.

Step 8 — Your implementation roadmap

I recommend working in this order so each stage builds on the previous one.


  Implementation checklist                                                       0 / 7 complete


        1. Basic server and MongoDB connection
          Initialize Express, connect to Atlas, and confirm the server responds.

       2. Mongoose schemas
       Define users and tasks, including defaults, required fields, and timestamps.

       3. Simple GET and POST endpoints
       Learn to retrieve and create documents, with validation and correct response formats.
       4. GET by ID, PUT, and DELETE
       Implement lookups, replacements, deletion, and missing-resource handling.

       5. Query parameters
      Add where, sort, select, skip, limit, and count using Mongoose.

       6. Two-way reference consistency
      Keep pendingTasks and assignedUser synchronized when creating, changing, completing, or deleting records.

        7. Deployment and testing
       Deploy to Render, run the autograder, and fix the reported failures.


What I would focus on first

Start by making sure you understand these five operations:


app.get(...)
app.post(...)
User.find(...)
new User(...)
await user.save()


They give you the foundation for most of the project.

Then focus on the more difficult topics: validation, Mongoose query parameters, two-way
relationships, and database transactions.

The most important distinction is that creating a working API endpoint is only the beginning. To
complete MP3, your endpoint must also return exactly the right status codes, protect the database
from invalid changes, and keep Users and Tasks consistent.

Since your assignment allows LLM assistance with disclosure, remember to preserve your AI chat
logs and declare the assistance in the submission form.
